WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.852
What happens inside a connected device?

2
00:00:03.232 --> 00:00:05.254
Let's follow the current, the

3
00:00:05.254 --> 00:00:06.652
pins and the numbers.

4
00:00:07.602 --> 00:00:09.864
A torch is a battery, a switch

5
00:00:09.864 --> 00:00:11.502
and a lamp in one loop.

6
00:00:11.882 --> 00:00:14.348
Close the switch, and current flows

7
00:00:14.348 --> 00:00:16.598
round the loop: the lamp lights.

8
00:00:16.978 --> 00:00:19.672
Open it, and the flow stops everywhere.

9
00:00:20.622 --> 00:00:22.248
An LED needs a resistor.

10
00:00:22.628 --> 00:00:25.148
Close the switch: the resistor takes

11
00:00:25.148 --> 00:00:27.572
one volt, the LED takes the other

12
00:00:27.572 --> 00:00:29.540
two, and ten milliamps flow.

13
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.036
That's just right.

14
00:00:31.986 --> 00:00:33.708
A light sensor's resistance

15
00:00:33.708 --> 00:00:35.160
falls in bright light.

16
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.126
In daylight the input reads high, so

17
00:00:38.126 --> 00:00:40.196
the program keeps the lamp off.

18
00:00:40.576 --> 00:00:43.624
When it gets dark, the reading drops, and

19
00:00:43.624 --> 00:00:45.748
the program switches the LED on.

20
00:00:46.698 --> 00:00:47.772
This is a Pico.

21
00:00:48.152 --> 00:00:49.562
Each pin has a name.

22
00:00:49.942 --> 00:00:52.642
The LED is wired to pin GP15 through a

23
00:00:52.642 --> 00:00:54.946
resistor, and the button to GP14.

24
00:00:55.326 --> 00:00:57.114
When the program sets GP15

25
00:00:57.114 --> 00:00:58.638
high, the LED lights.

26
00:00:59.588 --> 00:01:01.538
A sensor reads the classroom's

27
00:01:01.538 --> 00:01:03.422
temperature every ten minutes.

28
00:01:03.802 --> 00:01:06.478
The program compares each reading with a

29
00:01:06.478 --> 00:01:09.226
threshold: the fan goes on above thirty

30
00:01:09.226 --> 00:01:11.908
degrees, and off only below twenty-eight.

31
00:01:12.288 --> 00:01:14.454
Two thresholds stop it flickering.

32
00:01:15.404 --> 00:01:16.976
An ADC turns a voltage

33
00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:18.320
into a whole number.

34
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.388
With three bits it has only eight steps,

35
00:01:21.568 --> 00:01:24.202
so the reading jumps from step to step.

36
00:01:24.582 --> 00:01:26.778
Ten bits give 1024 steps, fine

37
00:01:26.778 --> 00:01:28.338
enough for most sensors.

38
00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:31.034
An AND gate gives 1 only

39
00:01:31.034 --> 00:01:32.606
when both inputs are 1.

40
00:01:32.986 --> 00:01:35.974
Watch the truth table fill in, row by row:

41
00:01:36.154 --> 00:01:38.452
only the last row lights the lamp.

42
00:01:39.402 --> 00:01:40.974
Eight bits make a byte.

43
00:01:41.354 --> 00:01:44.132
Each bit has a place value: one, two,

44
00:01:44.312 --> 00:01:47.060
four, up to one hundred and twenty-eight.

45
00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.240
The letter A is stored as

46
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.250
sixty-five: sixty-four plus one.

47
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.678
Current in a loop, pins with names,

48
00:01:54.858 --> 00:01:57.540
readings turned into numbers, and logic

49
00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.168
that decides: that's every smart device.
